WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.620
Abraham: So how does an
organization decide what type

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:04.140
of cyber testing they should do?

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:06.800
This will depend on many different things.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.370
Nicole: This may be related to
the size of the organization, the

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:14.660
experience of its IT department and
the nature of the business itself.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.639
Abraham: A Pen test is best
used when an organisation wants

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:22.150
to focus on vulnerabilities
in a new system or project.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:27.029
They are a more useful way to identify
specific vulnerabilities rather than

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.250
how they would respond to a real attack.

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:33.210
They can identify smaller
vulnerabilities that on their own

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:37.140
might seem insignificant in the scheme
of things, but these can be used by

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.190
attackers as an entry into a system.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:43.050
Nicole: By following the
processes hackers do pen testers

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.820
can find these points of entry.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:50.290
Pen testers will generate detailed reports
which meet the client’s specific needs

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.760
ranking and rating any vulnerabilities.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.419
However, if a pen test is not done
correctly, it can cause damage by

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:02.879
crashing servers, exposing sensitive
data or even corrupt crucial data.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:08.429
Therefore, it is imperative that
a pen tester can be trusted and

00:01:08.429 --> 00:01:11.399
understands the needs of the
organization they are working for.

00:01:12.789 --> 00:01:15.920
Abraham: Vulnerability scanning
is often used for checking systems

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.900
like web servers, VPN endpoints and
internet connections and as part of

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.650
the software development lifecycle.

00:01:23.309 --> 00:01:28.080
It can help identify vulnerabilities
early as many attacks use bots to search

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.090
for and exploit known vulnerabilities.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.200
Nicole: By using the same tools,
weaknesses can be discovered in

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.750
advance and if many vulnerabilities are
evident, it shows a system is flawed.

00:01:39.309 --> 00:01:43.820
Automated scans save time and money
and can be repeated regularly.

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:47.809
By doing this an organization
can demonstrate they are meeting

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.259
standards and GDPR regulations.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.500
Abraham: However, some vulnerabilities
will be missed as there are always

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:58.550
those that a scanner will not pick up
and it may bring up false positives

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.830
meaning that specialist help is
still needed to identify results.

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:06.729
Nicole: Red team testing is used
to create as near as possible

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.300
how real attackers would act.

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:13.600
It can’t replace vulnerability scans or
penetration tests, but is more useful

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.830
to see what would happen in the event
of a real attack and is a good way of

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:22.970
testing the ability of an IT security
team or Security Operations Centre to

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:25.209
detect particular types of attacks.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.470
Abraham: However, it only usually
targets the vulnerability needed

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:33.320
to achieve the goal of the attack
and may not show all issues.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:36.929
It can also involve the high
costs of using specialists.

00:02:37.929 --> 00:02:40.819
Nicole: Social engineering
testing covers areas that the

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.350
other forms of testing don’t.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:47.269
They are also very common forms of
attack, and many organisations will

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.109
fall foul of them at some time.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:54.160
This form of testing helps the workforce
in understanding the part they play in

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.819
the vulnerability of their system both in
their personal life as well as at work.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:03.630
However, this type of testing doesn’t
work in isolation and should be

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.549
combined with other forms of testing.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.219
Abraham: Cyber security testing
is only part of the process of

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.500
safeguarding a system or network.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.430
Once testing has been done this
should be followed up by mitigations

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:19.149
that respond to any risks that have
been identified during testing.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.269
So, what exactly are mitigations?

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:26.530
Nicole: Mitigations are actions that
can help reduce the potential damage.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.140
Let’s look at some of
these in more detail.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.679
Abraham: If hackers gain entry to
a system, the threat needs to be

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:36.229
stopped before it can escalate.

00:03:36.829 --> 00:03:39.829
Having a plan in place can help
with this by making critical

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:43.940
decisions such as taking a system
offline before damage escalates.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:48.069
A mitigation plan can help with
identifying who has control

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:51.149
in these circumstances and can
make the appropriate decisions.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.730
Early containment is key
in reducing any impact.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.640
Nicole: This can be done by
performing regular updates and

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.830
patching any known vulnerabilities.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:07.000
Keeping malware protection up to date
and installing firewalls to block

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.740
unsecure access along with ensuring a
robust password protection policy and

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:17.060
multi factor authentication, can also
help to mitigate any potential damage.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.889
Abraham: Limiting user’s permissions
by only allowing access to files

00:04:21.929 --> 00:04:25.300
and folders necessary for them
to do their work can also help.

00:04:26.009 --> 00:04:30.540
A user access system can also verify
the identity of users, stopping

00:04:30.559 --> 00:04:34.029
hackers from taking advantage of
uncontrolled administrative privileges.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:38.490
This can also help to protect from
insider or social engineering attacks.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:43.229
Nicole: Those with access to sensitive
or highly available data should be the

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:48.139
first to be identified as at risk and
this approach should then be implemented

00:04:48.139 --> 00:04:50.349
through the system and all other users.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:55.299
Abraham: And finally, the
vulnerability of employees rather

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.250
than hardware and software systems.

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:01.690
By training staff on how to mitigate
threats by being fully aware of what

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:05.349
role they play in the cyber security
of their organisation, can help to

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.169
reduce the issue of human error.

00:05:08.569 --> 00:05:13.010
Regular training in how and why
cyber security is important can help

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:16.930
to develop a culture of security
across all employees, by helping

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:20.290
to make them all aware of potential
attack by social engineering.

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.969
Nicole: Within a business or
organisation everyone has a role

00:05:23.969 --> 00:05:27.800
to play to make sure that cyber
security is given the importance

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:32.280
it deserves by making them aware of
the impact the lack of it can have.

